# Lock LF on files where CRLF would break execution.
# Shell scripts must keep \n shebangs and POSIX line breaks.
install/*.sh        text eol=lf
install/*.ps1       text eol=lf
.github/workflows/* text eol=lf

# Lock LF on example-bundle inputs. `corvid bundle verify` hashes
# every file under `bindings_rust/` (and the `verify.sh` /
# `corvid-bundle.toml` companions) byte-for-byte; if Git checks
# any of them out with CRLF on one host and LF on another, the
# recorded sha256 in `corvid-bundle.toml` matches on one platform
# and silently mismatches on the other. The Linux CI failure on
# `failing_rebuild` (BundleHashMismatch: bindings_rust expected
# `cb895287…` but found `17f7d62c…`) was exactly this — a
# CRLF-on-Windows recorded hash that no Linux checkout could
# match. Forcing LF here makes the recorded hashes
# platform-independent, the way they were always meant to be.
examples/**/bindings_rust/** text eol=lf
examples/**/verify.sh        text eol=lf
examples/**/corvid-bundle.toml text eol=lf
examples/_bundle_demo_common.sh text eol=lf

# Lock LF on Corvid source files and recorded traces. Prompt
# templates inside a `.cor` source are rendered verbatim into the
# trace's `rendered` field at recording time, and the replay
# engine compares that field against the live render byte-for-
# byte (after timestamp/line-ending normalization). When a
# recording was made on a Linux host (LF) and a replay runs on a
# Windows working tree with CRLF, the comparison's CRLF→LF
# normalization handles the difference at compare time, but
# locking LF on disk avoids the slop entirely and keeps the
# committed traces stable across platforms.
*.cor   text eol=lf
*.jsonl text eol=lf
